WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.072 --> 00:00:03.370
-Hello, everyone,
I´m Seth Meyers,

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:05.038
the series finale
of "Brooklyn Nine-Nine"

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:06.306
aired earlier tonight,

00:00:06.306 --> 00:00:08.241
and now we´re so happy
to be joined

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:10.310
by the show´s incredible cast.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.812
Dirk Blocker,
Joel McKinnon Miller,

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:15.181
Stephanie Beatriz,
Andre Braugher,

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:18.051
Andy Samberg,
Melissa Fumero,

00:00:18.051 --> 00:00:19.486
Joe Lo Truglio

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:23.356
and co-creator
and showrunner Dan Goor.

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:25.291
Thank you all for being here.

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:28.495
Congratulations on
the amazing finale,

00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:30.697
the amazing accomplishment
of so many seasons.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:34.267
I want to start by a quick shout
out to those who aren´t here.

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:36.403
Terry Crews
can not be with us tonight.

00:00:36.403 --> 00:00:39.139
We did want to make sure
his presence was felt.

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:41.274
We do have a cut out Terry,

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:44.511
and we´re very happy
with the technology

00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:47.647
that we´ve used
to make his pecs go.

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:49.349
-Ooh.
-Yeah, really good.

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:51.384
[ Laughter ]
Really good.

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:52.619
And that´s all computers.

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:54.421
It looked like a crew guy
ran behind him,

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:56.322
but that´s all...

00:00:56.322 --> 00:00:58.858
-Broke the bank on the seats.

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:02.996
-Yeah, and we also --
Chelsea Peretti

00:01:02.996 --> 00:01:06.066
cannot be here, but she did --
she sent a video.

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:08.802
So let´s take a look
at what Chelsea has to say.

00:01:08.802 --> 00:01:10.737
-♫ My bags were packed ♫

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:13.973
♫ I was ready to go ♫

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:15.842
♫ Jordan´s assistant
was going to take me ♫

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:18.278
♫ He was at the door ♫

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:24.551
♫ And then my son started
puking for five hours ♫

00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:29.189
♫ He´s better now,
but I missed the trip ♫

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:32.692
♫ I bought a dress where
the back won´t zip ♫

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:37.297
♫ The website said size down
because it runs large ♫

00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:38.832
♫ Not that large ♫

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:45.238
♫ I´m not leaving
on a jet plane ♫

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:49.109
♫ I´m stuck in my [bleep]
house again ♫

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:50.343
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:53.580
Well, good luck, everyone.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.715
Thanks for having me
in this capacity.

00:01:55.715 --> 00:01:57.183
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.183 --> 00:02:00.253
-They get a bomb shelter,
I think.

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:02.689
-What a voice.
-What a beautiful voice.

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:04.357
-I like that she didn´t let
television

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:06.192
slow down her -- her pace.

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:07.327
She didn´t speed it up for us.

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:09.062
-No, and we gave her --
She was like,

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:10.663
"How long should it be?"
And we said, "10 seconds,

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:12.632
not a second more."
She really took advantage.

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:15.668
-But I should milk it, though?

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:16.970
-So I think these, you know,

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:20.240
obviously finales
are incredibly emotional.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.910
Oftentimes, you see, you know,
other shows that are beloved.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.479
You see films
of the final table read.

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:27.614
What was it like for you guys?

00:02:27.614 --> 00:02:29.349
And was it hamstrung

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:31.084
because of this crazy era
we´re living through?

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:32.952
-Well, it was quite hamstrung.

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:34.320
It was on Zoom.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.289
-So, that was the final
table read was on Zoom.

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:39.993
-Last day together we were
in the middle of shooting

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:44.397
when we did it, so we were all
in our dressing rooms on Zoom.

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:45.732
So you could like hear people

00:02:45.732 --> 00:02:47.934
laughing across the hall
and stuff.

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:50.803
But you know, inevitably someone
has like a terrible delay

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:52.472
and it ruins it.

00:02:52.472 --> 00:02:55.275
I feel like, Joe, didn´t you
have like a major problem?

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:58.378
-I had a major technical issue
with my computer

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:03.950
in that it kept dropping out,
so we had to swap out a laptop

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.553
with a -- with like one of
the wardrobe assistants,

00:03:06.553 --> 00:03:09.822
came in and I was -- I was
in a bit of a panic

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:12.559
because it seemed to happen only
on Charles Boyle´s line.

00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:13.793
[ Laughter ]

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:15.962
-And it froze on your face
and you were like...

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:17.230
[ Laughter ]

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:20.099
Like, so upset about it.
-Pure terror, I´m sure.

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:23.636
-I feel like we all heard you
yelling in the hallway, right?

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:25.338
[Bleep]

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:26.839
-It´s out!

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:29.242
We got a laptop?!
-It´s the finale!

00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:30.977
I want to be emotional.

00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:32.545
-I was -- I was --
-Cold sweats.

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:33.947
-It sounded --
it seemed to me

00:03:33.947 --> 00:03:35.982
like you were just making
an acting choice,

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:37.250
like a really long pause.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:38.885
-Boyle just flips out.
-Yeah.

00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:41.154
-Dan, as like somebody
who obviously knows,

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:43.690
like obviously put all this time
into writing the finale,

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:44.891
you know this is, you know,

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:47.660
the final thing
everybody´s going to see,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.863
when you watch like
a terrible, tech-laden

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:54.434
you know,
errors-laden Zoom read through,

00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:56.035
do you least think there´s
going to be so much better

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:57.270
when it´s on TV?
-No.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.906
Was instantly so depressed.

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:03.509
I-I -- but also,
it should be said,

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:06.045
with the sole exception
of Charles Boyle

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:07.447
not really doing well,
it was not

00:04:07.447 --> 00:04:09.249
that much of a disaster.
-That´s right.

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:11.684
-And by not doing well, I mean,
just technically.

00:04:11.684 --> 00:04:14.454
You know, I´m constantly
in a fetal position,

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:16.055
pulling my hair out
and convinced

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:18.157
that we´ve destroyed
the legacy of the show.

00:04:18.157 --> 00:04:20.760
-Have you unfolded now
that the finale is there?

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.030
Do you feel like
you´re finally non-fetal again?

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.399
-I am semi non-fetal.

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:28.201
Yeah, I´m not.
I´m not fetal.

00:04:28.201 --> 00:04:29.569
-That´s really exciting, man.
-That´s hot.

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:32.071
That´s hot look for you.
-Thank you.

00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:34.741
-You guys had, I mean,
almost all of you

00:04:34.741 --> 00:04:36.843
had really emotional moments
in the finale.

00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:40.380
You guys had an intimate moment
in the finale.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.315
Are those moments --
does Dan writes

00:04:42.315 --> 00:04:43.783
specifically what he wants?

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:47.487
Or do you guys, sometimes
as actors, find your own way?

00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:50.290
-Anytime there is a Jake and Amy
kiss on the show,

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:53.459
Dan requests that it be tender.

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:55.662
And it´s the worst,
and he knows we hate it.

00:04:55.662 --> 00:04:57.597
-Hate it so much.
-He goes, "Just make sure

00:04:57.597 --> 00:04:59.198
it´s really tender."

00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:03.069
I´m like, "I want to quit
the show So bad right now."

00:05:03.069 --> 00:05:05.438
Like, you´re trying to act good.

00:05:05.438 --> 00:05:07.373
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:11.077
-As a fan of the show,
I want what the other fans want,

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:14.280
which is a kiss that is tender.
-[Sighs]

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.016
-And I put that in the stage
directions every single time.

00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:19.085
-I wish they didn´t push it
so hard in the promos

00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:20.586
like, "Tonight on
Brooklyn Nine-Nine,"

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:22.789
things get tender."
-Yeah.

00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:24.457
-Really a problem.
-I feel like Dan paid

00:05:24.457 --> 00:05:25.758
someone off at promo.

00:05:25.758 --> 00:05:28.695
-Do you have -- do you have
the sort of COVID police

00:05:28.695 --> 00:05:30.029
on those intimate moments?

00:05:30.029 --> 00:05:32.365
-Well, we hadn´t until
this last year, of course.

00:05:32.365 --> 00:05:34.567
But, yeah, we weren´t
planning on doing one.

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:37.136
But it was actually...
How did it go down?

00:05:37.136 --> 00:05:38.705
Dan wanted to add it on set.

00:05:38.705 --> 00:05:42.442
He did say he wanted it to be
tender, if anyone´s wondering.

00:05:42.442 --> 00:05:44.577
But we weren´t --
we thought we couldn´t do it.

00:05:44.577 --> 00:05:47.580
And then he went
to the COVID safety specialist.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.649
-Yeah, I said -- because
we had written the scene,

00:05:49.649 --> 00:05:51.384
there´s a very emotional scene
between the two of them.

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:54.354
They are fantastic in it,
and it felt so strange

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:57.123
that we hadn´t written
that they should kiss.

00:05:57.123 --> 00:05:58.558
And I said, "Can we add a kiss?"

00:05:58.558 --> 00:06:01.294
And the director was like,
absolutely not. COVID.

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:02.462
They cannot kiss.

00:06:02.462 --> 00:06:04.764
If they kiss,
they have to both --

00:06:04.764 --> 00:06:06.132
We have to get it approved
in advance,

00:06:06.132 --> 00:06:09.035
they have to rapid test
and they have to mouthwash

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:10.336
immediately beforehand.

00:06:10.336 --> 00:06:12.438
Rinse with Listerine,
like a powerful Listerine,

00:06:12.438 --> 00:06:14.540
more powerful than normal.
-Like Glycerin.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:15.842
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:16.976
And I was like, "Okay."

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:19.379
And all of a sudden,
the COVID guy,

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:22.915
Sean walked up and he said,

00:06:22.915 --> 00:06:24.650
"They can kiss."

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:26.586
And we were like, it´s a go,

00:06:26.586 --> 00:06:27.787
and they kissed,
it was tender.

00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:28.955
-And then it was
a pretty massive

00:06:28.955 --> 00:06:30.656
breakout, right?
You guys had to shut it down.

00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:32.191
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:35.027
-For sure.
Took down the whole lot.

00:06:35.027 --> 00:06:37.997
It spread to other shows.
It was [bleep] up.

00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:39.298
-First case of Delta in L.A.

00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:41.601
[ Laughter ]

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:43.302
-Andre, we´ve talked
before on the show.

00:06:43.302 --> 00:06:46.539
The funniest thing
that Andy ever told me

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:50.376
was that he was going to be
in a television show with you.

00:06:50.376 --> 00:06:52.178
-Yes, it was funny to me, too.
-Yeah, it was funny

00:06:52.178 --> 00:06:54.680
to you, too. I can only imagine
how funny it was for you.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.383
I was actually thinking about
this when the the late great

00:06:57.383 --> 00:06:58.818
Ed Asner passed away

00:06:58.818 --> 00:07:00.820
because he played Lou Grant
as his comedy character,

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:04.524
and then he played it
as a dramatic character.

00:07:04.524 --> 00:07:07.160
And obviously, you played a
different character on this show

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:08.494
than you did on homicide,

00:07:08.494 --> 00:07:10.730
but there was
a dramatic police show

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:12.899
and now this comedy police show.

00:07:12.899 --> 00:07:15.468
Do you feel like this is a sort
of perfect bookend to these --

00:07:15.468 --> 00:07:16.803
these two acts in your career?

00:07:16.803 --> 00:07:19.439
-I do feel that way
in a certain way.

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:22.542
You know,
I´d grown tired of playing cops.

00:07:22.542 --> 00:07:23.676
You know what I mean?
I don´t.

00:07:23.676 --> 00:07:25.411
My wife says,
you´re the perfect cop

00:07:25.411 --> 00:07:26.913
and I don´t see
what she´s talking about.

00:07:26.913 --> 00:07:30.583
But -- but I´m often cast
in that way.

00:07:30.583 --> 00:07:33.319
And this was
the perfect opportunity

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:36.289
to -- to spin it
in a certain way, you know,

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:37.990
and to explore
something different.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:40.426
And -- and exactly
as I had hoped,

00:07:40.426 --> 00:07:41.527
it was transformational.

00:07:41.527 --> 00:07:43.095
I mean,
in terms of my career,

00:07:43.095 --> 00:07:45.264
in terms of my --
my acting skill,

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:48.034
in terms of my joy, you know,
in front of the camera.

00:07:48.034 --> 00:07:50.169
So it´s been a great experience,
you know?

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:53.639
-And you know the other thing
that was a very famous chapter

00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:55.341
of this show
was that it was canceled

00:07:55.341 --> 00:07:57.944
and then sort of brought back
by the fans.

00:07:57.944 --> 00:08:01.113
Was it exciting to go out
on your own terms this time?

00:08:01.113 --> 00:08:02.181
-It was amazing.
-Yeah.

00:08:02.181 --> 00:08:04.650
-I mean, I think we all really
feel

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:08.354
exceptionally proud of the work
that we did this last season.

00:08:08.354 --> 00:08:13.493
And like with all the strange
challenges and the, like,

00:08:13.493 --> 00:08:15.127
all of the challenges,
the writing challenges,

00:08:15.127 --> 00:08:16.896
the acting challenges,
the crew challenges,

00:08:16.896 --> 00:08:18.731
but it felt really,
I don´t know.

00:08:18.731 --> 00:08:21.000
Felt, like you said,
joyful.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:24.303
It felt really joyful the way
that we finished it.

00:08:24.303 --> 00:08:26.339
-Well, it felt that way
to watch as well.

00:08:26.339 --> 00:08:27.373
We´ll be right back with more

00:08:27.373 --> 00:08:28.674
from the cast
of "Brooklyn Nine-Nine."

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:31.177
But first,
Lin-Manuel Miranda´s

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:35.348
musical take on the
"Brooklyn Nine-Nine" theme song.

00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:36.616
♫ Brooklyn Nine-Nine ♫

00:08:36.616 --> 00:08:37.416
♫ Nine-Nine ♫

00:08:37.416 --> 00:08:38.818
♫ Brooklyn Nine-Nine ♫

00:08:38.818 --> 00:08:39.952
♫ Nine-Nine ♫

00:08:39.952 --> 00:08:42.355
♫ Everyone who sees this show ♫

00:08:42.355 --> 00:08:43.990
♫ Agrees it rocks ♫

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:46.025
♫ It´s on NBC ♫

00:08:46.025 --> 00:08:48.461
♫ ´Cause it´s too good
for Fox ♫

00:08:48.461 --> 00:08:49.929
♫ Brooklyn Nine-Nine ♫

00:08:49.929 --> 00:08:50.730
♫ Sad to see you go ♫

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:52.131
♫ Brooklyn Nine-Nine ♫

00:08:52.131 --> 00:08:52.999
♫ You´re a good show ♫

00:08:52.999 --> 00:08:54.867
♫ Andre Braugher rules ♫

00:08:54.867 --> 00:08:56.602
I love you.
-Welcome back.

00:08:56.602 --> 00:08:59.906
We are here with the wonderful
cast of "Brooklyn Nine-Nine."

00:08:59.906 --> 00:09:02.742
Dan, I´m wondering how you came
up with the idea for the show

00:09:02.742 --> 00:09:07.013
and how you first
approached Andy with the pitch.

00:09:07.013 --> 00:09:09.615
-So I used to work on
"Parks and Recreation"

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:11.684
with the wonderful Mike Schur,

00:09:11.684 --> 00:09:14.020
who you are very familiar with.
-Yes.

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:15.821
-And I was there for five years,

00:09:15.821 --> 00:09:18.190
and we decided we wanted
to create a show together.

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.726
So we had this idea
and we were trying to figure out

00:09:20.726 --> 00:09:23.396
who the perfect
lead actor would be.

00:09:23.396 --> 00:09:25.164
And then Andy, it turns out,

00:09:25.164 --> 00:09:27.900
had just left
"Saturday Night Live"

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:29.235
and he was in Los Angeles.

00:09:29.235 --> 00:09:32.572
So Mike and I in what is
the most Hollywood experience

00:09:32.572 --> 00:09:36.309
I´ve ever had,
went to the Villa

00:09:36.309 --> 00:09:39.245
that Andy and Lonely Island
we´re renting.

00:09:39.245 --> 00:09:40.746
-That´s right.
-A villa...

00:09:40.746 --> 00:09:42.448
[ Laughter ]

00:09:42.448 --> 00:09:45.318
in order to record
their third album.

00:09:45.318 --> 00:09:47.119
-I think I was at that villa.
Was I at that villa?

00:09:47.119 --> 00:09:48.554
-You definitely visited
said villa.

00:09:48.554 --> 00:09:49.555
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:09:49.555 --> 00:09:50.957
-It was
a real Hollywood villa.

00:09:50.957 --> 00:09:53.259
-Oh, yes.

00:09:53.259 --> 00:09:54.493
So, where did you take
this meeting?

00:09:54.493 --> 00:09:55.661
At the pool?
Where did you?

00:09:55.661 --> 00:09:56.796
-It was poolside.

00:09:56.796 --> 00:10:00.433
I brought Mike and Dan
out by the pool.

00:10:00.433 --> 00:10:03.102
We had refreshments.
They pitched me on the show.

00:10:03.102 --> 00:10:06.439
The character,
his name was Jake.

00:10:06.439 --> 00:10:08.541
-I´m so grossed out right now.
-Why

00:10:08.541 --> 00:10:10.476
-Did you take a lot of calls
during the meeting?

00:10:10.476 --> 00:10:12.111
-I took many calls
during the meeting.

00:10:12.111 --> 00:10:14.180
